(6915009, 1041703, 10785, '<td valign="top">Congrats on your first Ribbon! A new member of the elite!! :)</td>', 'Congrats on your first Ribbon! A new member of the elite!! :)', '2012-11-28 09:18:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(6915013, 1041955, 131871, '<td valign="top">Congrats!! I absolutely LOVE this photo. Your model is gorgeous and the composition is interesting and beautiful. This is about the 10th time I have looked at this photo so far today. I just love it!</td>', 'Congrats!! I absolutely LOVE this photo. Your model is gorgeous and the composition is interesting and beautiful. This is about the 10th time I have looked at this photo so far today. I just love it!', '2012-11-28 09:27:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(6915015, 1041856, 10785, '<td valign="top">Camera: Canon EOS-5D <br/>Lens: Canon EF 100mm f/2.8L Macro IS USM<br/>Aperture: 2.8<br/>ISO: 3200<br/>Shutter: 1/60<br/> <br/>Just had to revisit this to check the exif. It shows as possible camera shake. With a shutter of 1/60 at a focal length of 100mm that is definitely too slow of a shutter. I think the rule of thumb for handheld in low light is to have a shutter speed at least 2x focal length. You may be able to get away with 1.5x shutter speed. (I run into this problem all the time because my camera body only goes to ISO 1600!) :)</td>', 'Camera: Canon EOS-5D Lens: Canon EF 100mm f/2.8L Macro IS USMAperture: 2.8ISO: 3200Shutter: 1/60 Just had to revisit this to check the exif. It shows as possible camera shake. With a shutter of 1/60 at a focal length of 100mm that is definitely too slow of a shutter. I think the rule of thumb for handheld in low light is to have a shutter speed at least 2x focal length. You may be able to get away with 1.5x shutter speed. (I run into this problem all the time because my camera body only goes to ISO 1600!) :)', '2012-11-28 09:29:16.000000', NULL, 0),
